{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And the ground that was under them split, and the earth opened her mouth and swallowed them.</b> Ramban wrote: the reason it needed to say 'and the earth opened' after it had already mentioned 'and the ground split' is so that you should not say that the splitting of the ground was according to the nature and custom of the world — for there are lands that split because of the earthquake called טרימוט\"ו, and the matter would be entirely natural with no miracle at all. Therefore it afterward comes to tell us 'and the earth opened her mouth,' to explain that it was a great miracle and a thing the like of which had never been: the earth opened her mouth and swallowed them, and afterward the place was covered over — this is what it says, ״ותכס עליהם הארץ״ — and this is not a natural phenomenon, for in the splitting of the ground in certain lands according to the custom of the world, after that place splits it remains open; but here, after the splitting, it says that the earth opened her mouth and swallowed them and returned to cover over them, and this was a great miracle among the renowned miracles. And because of this the verse used the language of creation: ״ואם בריאה יברא ה׳״ — which is the language of bringing forth something from nothing — and this renewed phenomenon was an immense wonder and a great innovation like the innovation of bringing forth something from nothing. End of his words." "<b>And all the men who belonged to Korah.</b> These are the members of his household and all who were subject to his authority. His sons too are included among those swallowed by the earth, but they did not die, as it is written (Numbers 26:11): ״ובני קרח לא מתו״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: a place was partitioned for them in Gehinnom and they sat upon it. And it could have said ״ואת כל האנשים״ as it said above, ״כי נאצו האנשים האלה״, but it mentioned all who were swallowed by the name אדם on account of their punishment, that they were swallowed into the ground (אדמה)." "<b>And all the substance.</b> The verse told us that the bodies and the houses and the money were swallowed, for this great punishment fell upon everything. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: even a borrowed needle of theirs in the hand of one of Israel would roll and go down and be swallowed into the ground. And as for what it says ״אשר לקרח״ and did not explicitly mention the swallowing of Korah himself — there is no need, for since Dathan and Abiram with the members of their households, their wives and their children and their little ones, also the members of Korah's household were all swallowed on account of Korah's sin, all the more so he himself, who was the head of the affair and the root of the conflict; and although it did not explain this here, in another place we find it written explicitly (Numbers 26:10): ״ותבלע אותם ואת קרח״. And we have already found the like of this (Exodus 15:4): ״מרכבות פרעה וחילו ירה בים״, and the verse did not explain the drowning of Pharaoh, but from a fortiori the matter is clarified; and although the verse left it unspecified and did not explain his drowning, King David, peace be upon him, came and explained the matter, as he said (Psalms 136:15): ״ונער פרעה וחילו בים סוף״. And the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory is that Korah was both swallowed and burned, for since he was the cause of the conflict two punishments were carried out upon him, and this is from what is written (Numbers 26:10): ״ותבלע אותם ואת קרח במות העדה באכול האש״ — the verse teaches you that Korah was swallowed, and he was also in the consuming of the fire, for the consuming of the fire extended to Korah as well. And the reason it did not mention him explicitly here with the burning of the two hundred and fifty — from a fortiori the matter is known, for since all of them were offerers of incense and each and every one with his firepan in his hand, he too was burned with them; and although it did not mention this, there is no need, for the punishment of all of them was only on his account. Or one may say that all the instances of the word את are inclusive, for in saying ״ותאכל את החמשים ומאתים איש״, it comes to include Korah."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {2 "The term טרימוט\"ו in comment 0 is a transliterated Old French word (cf. Ramban's commentary ad loc.); left untranslated as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:28.104539691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}